The code statements that lead to the bug are:
memcpy(n, dm_block_data(child), dm_bm_block_size(dm_tm_get_bm(info->tm)));
dm_tm_dec(info->tm, dm_block_location(child));

Reason:
When the internal btree node has a single entry and its child is shared (reference count > 1), copying the child's content into the parent creates an additional pointer to each grandchild without incrementing their reference counts. The subsequent `dm_tm_dec` only decrements the child's reference count (which stays above zero because it's shared), leaving the grandchild reference counts undercounted relative to the actual number of pointers. This leads to a refcount underflow when the space map later tries to decrement a grandchild block.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
r = dm_tm_block_is_shared(info->tm, b, &is_shared);
if (r)
	return r;
if (is_shared)
	inc_children(info->tm, dm_block_data(child), vt);

Reason:
Before copying the child's content into the parent, the fix checks whether the child block is shared. If it is shared, the `inc_children` function increments the reference counts of all grandchildren (the entries in the child node). This ensures that the new pointers in the parent are properly accounted for, preventing the mismatch between pointer count and reference count that caused the underflow.

